1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
sin and cos. What are they actually FOR?

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,200
They turn an angle into a place.

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,600
A circle. Radius one. That is the whole set-up.

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,000
Turn by any angle θ. Where does the point land?

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,800
How far ACROSS it went — 0.8. That is cos θ.

6
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,600
How far UP it went — 0.6. That is sin θ.

7
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,000
That is not a fact about cos and sin. That IS them.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,800
0.8 goes into cos θ. 0.6 goes into sin θ.

9
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:21,600
Width and height of one turn. Nothing else.

10
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,800
Same angle — but the real arrow is 5 long, not 1.

11
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,200
That length is r. Same circle, five times bigger.

12
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,600
x = 5 × 0.8 = 4.   y = 5 × 0.6 = 3.

13
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,000
An angle went in. A place came out.

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:32,800
Every game character, every robot arm, every rotating screen.

15
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,000
We learned this at school.

16
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,200
Nobody ever said what for.

17
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,800
Send this to your school friend — and tell them THIS is how it's solved.

18
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,000
Follow for the math behind AI. @observer.collapse
